<VOTABLE version="1.4" xmlns="http://www.ivoa.net/xml/VOTable/v1.3" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.ivoa.net/xml/VOTable/v1.3 http://www.ivoa.net/xml/VOTable/VOTable-1.4.xsd">
 <RESOURCE type="results">
  <INFO ID="aperture_arcsec" name="aperture_arcsec" value="24.6"/>
  <INFO ID="method" name="method" value="Per frame: a 6 arcmin cutout at IRSA&apos;s predicted comet position, a fixed 4 pixel (24.6 arcsec) radius aperture, sky from the median of a 12 to 20 pixel annulus, times the pixel solid angle to get microjanskys; error from the annulus scatter; wavelength from each frame&apos;s per-pixel wavelength map at the measured centroid. Qualitatively this can be compared with Lisse et al. (arXiv:2601.06759), the SPHEREx team&apos;s 3I/ATLAS spectrum, whose bands (water, C-H, CO2, CO) are the ones listed under bands; no numbers from that paper are used here."/>
  <INFO ID="ephemeris" name="ephemeris" value="IRSA comet3iatlas table"/>
  <INFO ID="caveat1" name="caveat1" value="The comet is an extended coma, so the flux depends on the aperture: these are 4 pixel (24.6 arcsec) radius sums with no aperture correction, and a different aperture gives a different spectrum."/>
  <INFO ID="caveat2" name="caveat2" value="This is flux, not reflectance: the solar spectrum and the changing comet-Sun and comet-SPHEREx distances are not divided out."/>
  <INFO ID="caveat3" name="caveat3" value="No water-ice claim is made from the December data."/>
  <INFO ID="caveat4" name="caveat4" value="Each frame sees one wavelength at a different time, so rotation and activity changes between frames are mixed into the spectrum along with wavelength."/>
  <INFO ID="caveat5" name="caveat5" value="The August 2025 rows come from a different date, when the comet was much fainter and farther away, so they sit 10 to 50 times lower and should be read as a separate epoch, not part of the December spectrum."/>
  <INFO ID="caveat6" name="caveat6" value="The sky annulus (12 to 20 pixels) can contain outer coma, so the fluxes may read low."/>
